"407 PART VII Proposed Federal Statutes Regulating Civil Procedure TABLE OF CONTENTS Page Proposed Protecting Businesses From Frivolous COVID Lawsuits Act of 2025 407 Proposed Protect the Gig Economy Act of 2025 407 Proposed Litigation Transparency Act of 2025 408 Proposed Justice for All Act of 2025 409 Proposed No Rogue Rulings Act of 2025 410 Proposed Court Shopping Deterrence Act 411 Proposed Restraining Judicial Insurrectionist Act of 2025 411 Proposed Nationwide Injunction Abuse Prevention Act of 2025 412 Proposed Sunshine in the Courtroom Act 412 Proposed Cameras in the Courtroom Act 414 Proposed Judicial Relief Clarification Act of 2025 414 Proposed Protecting Our Courts from Foreign Manipulation Act 415 Proposed Wrongful Injunction Accountability Act 417 Proposed One Big Beautiful Bill Act 417 Proposed Federal Prosecutorial Accountability Act 418 Proposed Diversity Jurisdiction Inflation Adjustment Act 418 Proposed Protecting Businesses From Frivolous COVID Lawsuits Act of 2025 H.R. 99, 119th Cong., 1st Sess., was introduced in the House of Representatives and referred to the House Judiciary Committee on January 3, 2025. The bill would require a specific jury instruction in federal civil actions for damages based on negligence arising from COVID-19 transmissions. It provides: In a Federal civil action that includes a claim alleging negligence arising from the transmission of COVID-19 and a request for damages, the court shall instruct a jury that— (1) the liability standard is the reasonable person standard, (2) a person is negligent if the person either does something that a reasonably careful person would not do in the same situation, or fails to do something that a reasonably careful person would do, in the same situation, (3) the act of opening a business, by itself, shall be considered to be reasonable as a matter of law, and (4) the negligence may not be found solely on the basis of holding oneself open for business. Proposed Protect the Gig Economy Act of 2025 H.R. 100, 119th Cong., 1st Sess., was introduced in the House of Representatives and referred to the House Judiciary Committee on January 3, 2025. The stated purpose is to “protect the ‘gig economy’ and small businesses that operate in large part through contractor services from the threat of costly class action litigation.” It provides: 408 Rule 23(a) of the Federal Rules of Civil Procedure is amended— (1) in paragraph (3), by striking “and” at the end; (2) in paragraph (4), by striking the period at the end and inserting “; and”; and (3) by inserting after paragraph (4) the following: “(3) the claim does not allege the misclassification of employees as independent contractors.”. Proposed Litigation Transparency Act of 2025 H.R. 1109, 119th Cong., Ist Sess., was introduced in the House of Representatives and referred to the House Judiciary Committee on February 7, 2025. The bill would require disclosure of third-party litigation funding in civil actions. The bill would add Title 28, section 1660, as follows: Section 1660. Third-party beneficiary disclosure (a) In GeNERAL.—Except as provided in subsection (b), in any civil action, a party or any counsel of record for a party shall—"